plan React
title   React ownership, attachment and passive views
status  proposed
goal
    Components read consistent data and keep stable actor handles without starting work in render.
outcomes
    O1  Provider and Runtime ownership
        flow-state/react exposes <FlowProvider setup={setup}> and <FlowProvider runtime={runtime}>.
        With setup, the Provider constructs and owns one Runtime per setup scope: a new setup value
        with the same scope keeps the existing Runtime, while a changed scope (sign-in, sign-out,
        account switch) constructs a fresh Runtime and disposes the old one once it has been unused
        for longer than the 100 ms grace, so Strict Mode remounts keep it. With runtime, the host
        owns construction and disposal and the Provider never disposes. Either form renders children
        immediately without suspending or showing a readiness fallback. Its first effect calls
        ready(), so a browser app restores without a separate host call; unmount cancels only that
        observer, not shared readiness. Readiness failure reaches a React error boundary on the next
        render. Hooks use the nearest Provider; unrelated nested Runtimes never share data or
        ownership. useView rejects a foreign Runtime's handle with ForeignActorRef before
        subscribing. An old scope's Runtime is never left running. Isolation follows
        @plan.Runtime.O7. All useView reads in one render see one consistent version across actors
        and store entries.
    O2  Hooks and the first render
        useActor(machine, options?) returns one fresh local actor handle per component incarnation.
        useActorByRef(ref, options) ensures a shared actor and returns a handle for a non-null ref;
        any options object, including {}, selects ensure. Declared input must have its exact type.
        useActorByRef(ref) only looks up and returns Actor | null; missing or disposed means null. A
        null ref returns null and starts nothing. Invalid, foreign, opaque-local or mismatched refs
        reject with ForeignActorRef. Lookup neither subscribes nor retains an actor. Before
        attachment, ensure returns a stable handle with a pure snapshot: default states,
        initial(input) memory, available passive provider context, active lifecycle, no issues,
        active resource lanes Pending with undefined data or a pure placeholder; inactive lanes
        absent. Pending lanes alone do not mark optimistic writes pending. Attached actors show
        committed data. Render registers nothing, subscribes to nothing, starts no work, writes no
        storage and emits no evidence, including lazy adoption. initial must be pure: it may run in
        discarded renders and on both server and client, so it reads no clock or randomness. Fresh
        attachment keeps the prepared memory; restored actor precedence is @plan.Persistence.O4.
        Server and hydration snapshots follow @plan.ServerRendering.O4. Handles, sends and typed
        event constructors stay stable for the same identity. Equivalent refs resolve the same
        actor; changing the ref resolves the new identity. Local input is read once; changing local
        machine or input requires a keyed remount and emits a development diagnostic. Shared input
        never resets an existing actor; conflicting ref options follow @plan.Runtime.O3.
    O3  Attach, detach, Strict Mode and grace
        After commit and readiness, an ensuring hook attaches the same handle through the Runtime.
        It uses current provider context without replaying initial, input or onContext work. Lazy
        admission follows @plan.LazyFeatures.O2; failed admission leaves no partial actor. Before
        attachment, at most 64 sends buffer in FIFO order; send 65 throws
        FlowError(AdmissionOverloaded) without accepting the event. Attachment delivers accepted
        sends exactly once through @plan.Runtime.O4. Sends through a handle whose actor is disposed
        are dropped as SendTargetAbsent; discarded renders never deliver their buffered events to
        another component incarnation. Local cleanup leaves the actor active for 100 milliseconds on
        the Runtime Clock. Reattach before expiry keeps the actor and cancels disposal; expiry
        disposes it through the Runtime. Work continues during grace. Strict Mode cleanup/setup
        never duplicates work or loses the actor. Shared ensuring hooks count as uses until cleanup;
        retention follows @plan.Runtime.O3. Dropping a JavaScript handle disposes nothing. Explicit
        disposal is visible to all users; a mounted ensuring hook re-ensures a fresh incarnation
        under @plan.Runtime.O3. Unmount while awaiting readiness never admits later. Reattach during
        disposal waits for cleanup; failed cleanup surfaces through @O5. React Activity hiding
        beyond grace disposes local actors; reveal creates a fresh one. Shared refs with retain are
        the way to keep state across hiding.
    O4  Eligibility in views
        useView(actor, select) accepts an exact handle and preserves the selector's exact return
        type. Nullable lookup must be branched first; Machines, refs and comparator arguments are
        rejected. Selectors read frozen state, memory, context, issues, lifecycle and activities. A
        disposed snapshot keeps its last state, memory and context with lifecycle disposed. State
        tokens narrow snapshot types; check(s, event) follows @plan.Machines.O4. Activity data
        follows @plan.Operations.O6. descriptor.get and Entity.get are passive: reading never
        fetches, mutates or retains data. Selectors start no work and acquire no ownership.
        Transactions have no view reader. Changes to currently selected descriptor keys update the
        view even without an actor change; conditional reads replace those dependencies. Selected
        values compare with Equal.equals, and unchanged lanes keep identity. Changing selector
        identity does not resubscribe to the actor.
    O5  Errors in views
        A selector exception reaches the nearest React error boundary. Reading unchanged relevant
        data rethrows the same exception without reevaluation; a later matching publication retries,
        while unrelated updates do not. An attached view failure leaves committed data and siblings
        intact. Initial attachment or hydration validation failure aborts admission without a
        partial actor. Readiness, admission, lazy adoption, provenance and cleanup failures retain
        the FlowError classification of @plan.Diagnostics.O1 and rethrow on the next render.
        Boundary reset allows adoption retry on a later attachment, never by loading during render.
        Resource failure lanes and typed machine errors remain view data; they do not automatically
        throw. Unknown commit reconciliation follows @plan.Operations.O3. Hooks never suspend.
verification
    V1  Type/mount tests prove exact hook types, Provider isolation, readiness and boundary errors.
    V2  Render tests prove pure snapshots, stable handles, ref changes and no discarded-render work.
    V3  Attach tests prove 64 buffered sends, FIFO delivery once, current context and unmount
        safety.
    V4  Lifecycle tests prove grace, Strict Mode, retention, re-ensure and cleanup failures.
    V5  View tests prove consistent passive reads, key updates, equality and isolated error retries.
    V6  Hydration tests prove matching first HTML and no duplicated work.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        The private attachment and subscription implementation; how selector failures are cached for
        unchanged relevant data; development diagnostic wording for local machine/input changes;
        lifecycle race generation and browser test coverage.
